language-derived-from-the-record-10 = unlines (cons "**8. Vision and the verbal quotient.** A participant's record is everything it has received; an occurrence of a word is an event of the record the participant identifies as that word, and its context is the record around it; the meaning of a present occurrence is what followed the past occurrences whose contexts are identified with the present one, transported to now. Occurrences are physical events in a participant's sensory stream, and a spoken word's occurrences arrive with what was seen at the time: light striking the retina, which is brain tissue. A record's contexts are mostly visual. Reading is a visual event identified with auditory occurrences, a path between occurrence types across senses: synthetic synesthesia, which is why written language programs brains. The verbal quotient is the map from the record R to R_verbal that forgets all but the words. Identifications among words whose verbal contexts match survive it; selections that only vision made do not. A model trained on the human superorganism's R_verbal has determinate meaning exactly where verbal context alone selects, and indeterminate meaning where seeing selected: the descent theorem (a law descends through an observation exactly when equal observations are followed by equal ones) through the verbal quotient. That is why text-only models carry real meaning, and exactly where they lose it." nil)
